{
  "_about": "Known-answer tests for Ed25519-Blake2b, the signature scheme Nano uses: RFC 8032 Ed25519 with Blake2b-512 in place of SHA-512. The signing cases are generated by the tools in verify/; the ICP signature came from the canister in signer/ and the genesis signature from nano-node; the two divergence cases are constructed arithmetically -- S + L from the genesis signature, and the order-8 case on the keypair docs.nano.org publishes. The signing cases use the deterministic RFC 8032-style nonce; nano-node randomises its nonce, so a node-style signer will produce a different valid signature. Of the fields here only public_key, clamped and a are reproduced by such a signer: nonce, r and R depend on the nonce, and challenge, k and S depend on R. Verify those cases rather than comparing bytes, or check challenge and k against the R given here. All cases are re-checked by verify/tests/known_answers.rs on every cargo test, so the file cannot drift from the tools.",
  "_source": "https://github.com/xnoforge/ed25519-blake2b-testvector",
  "_licence": "MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Bernhard Klingseisen",
  "_version": "2026-11-21",
  "_warning": "The private keys here are test material published for this purpose. The accounts they derive are real Nano accounts. Do not fund them.",
  "_fields": {
    "rfc8032": "expected outcome of standard Ed25519 verification: valid, invalid, or error",
    "ed25519_blake2b": "expected outcome under the strict Ed25519-Blake2b rule implemented here: canonical S and a decompressable R are required. This is NOT what a Nano node answers for every input -- see ed25519_blake2b_donna for that",
    "intermediates": "every value in the signing equation, for localising a mistake",
    "ed25519_blake2b_donna": "expected outcome under nano-node's rule as modelled here from ed25519.c: S reduced mod L with only the top three bits of its last byte tested, and R never decompressed. No donna code runs in this repository, so this column is a reading of that source, not its output. It is the column an implementation aiming at consensus must match, and it differs from ed25519_blake2b for the S + L case.",
    "ed25519_blake2b_cofactored": "expected outcome when the same strict parse rules are kept but RFC 8032 §5.1.7's cofactored equation is used instead: [8][S]B == [8]R + [8][k]A. Nano, ed25519-donna and ed25519-dalek all use the cofactorless form. This column differs from ed25519_blake2b for the order-8 case below. An implementation aiming at consensus must match ed25519_blake2b_donna, not this column; it is here so that the choice of equation is testable rather than assumed."
  },
