    {
      "name": "genesis signature with S replaced by S + L",
      "public_key": "E89208DD038FBB269987689621D52292AE9C35941A7484756ECCED92A65093BA",
      "message": "991CF190094C00F0B68E2E5F75F6BEE95A2E0BD93CEAA4A6734DB9F19B728948",
      "signature": "9F0C933C8ADE004D808EA1985FA746A7E95BA2A38F867640F53EC8F180BDFE9E19E65D3BC8DF38BCC9F3DA1D99300ADA8E46DBA03E523A7B5A19E4B6EB12BB12",
      "rfc8032": "error",
      "ed25519_blake2b": "error",
      "ed25519_blake2b_donna": "valid",
      "ed25519_blake2b_cofactored": "error",
      "note": "Consensus-critical divergence. S + L is not canonical, so this repository's verifiers reject it as malformed. nano-node accepts it: ed25519-donna only tests the top three bits of S (ed25519.c:111) and then reduces mod L, and byte 63 of S + L is 0x12 here, so the test passes and the equation holds. An implementation aiming at consensus compatibility must match the node, not the stricter rule."
    },
    {
      "name": "R carrying a point of order 8: cofactorless against cofactored",
      "public_key": "3068BB1CA04525BB0E416C485FE6A67FD52540227D267CC8B6E8DA958A7FA039",
      "message": "991CF190094C00F0B68E2E5F75F6BEE95A2E0BD93CEAA4A6734DB9F19B728948",
      "signature": "D6FB15FA6CF2EB955BA5103501B8D79CE326C82FA044BA7B2C45A52FCFC712671A5EF4B6B139832616663C9CC0FEACEB24AEF0B42A7A4BBE8670369C00EDCC0E",
      "rfc8032": "invalid",
      "ed25519_blake2b": "invalid",
      "ed25519_blake2b_donna": "invalid",
      "ed25519_blake2b_cofactored": "valid",
      "note": "The cofactor divergence, which is the other classic Ed25519 interoperability split besides non-canonical S. The strict rule rejects this signature and so does the node's rule, both applying the cofactorless equation, [S]B = R + [k]A as an equality of points. A cofactored verifier, checking [8][S]B = [8]R + [8][k]A instead, accepts it -- the difference [S]B - [k]A - R is a point of order 8 rather than the identity, and multiplying by the cofactor sends it to zero. The public key is the keypair docs.nano.org publishes; R was built as rB plus the point of order 8 encoded C7176A703D4DD84FBA3C0B760D10670F2A2053FA2C39CCC64EC7FD7792AC037A, which point-checks reports as R not being torsion free. Order 8 rather than 4 so that the case also fixes the cofactor: a verifier multiplying by 4 rejects this signature. The rfc8032 column is not informative here: under SHA-512 the challenge is a different scalar, so that verifier rejects for ordinary reasons."
    }
  ],
